In the rotating frame, the Coriolis acceleration involves the velocity,
\[
-2\boldsymbol{\omega}\times\dot{\mathbf r}
=\left(2\omega\dot y,-2\omega\dot x,0\right).
\]
Therefore,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ddot x&=-GM_1\frac{x+\alpha R}{\rho_1^3}
       -GM_2\frac{x-(1-\alpha)R}{\rho_2^3}
       +\omega^2x+2\omega\dot y,\\
\ddot y&=-GM_1\frac{y}{\rho_1^3}
       -GM_2\frac{y}{\rho_2^3}
       +\omega^2y-2\omega\dot x.
\end{aligned}
\]

For the given effective potential,
\[
U=-\frac{GM_1}{\rho_1}-\frac{GM_2}{\rho_2}
-\frac{\omega^2}{2}(x^2+y^2),
\]
its derivatives are
\[
\frac{\partial U}{\partial x}
=\frac{GM_1(x+\alpha R)}{\rho_1^3}
+\frac{GM_2[x-(1-\alpha)R]}{\rho_2^3}
-\omega^2x,
\]
\[
\frac{\partial U}{\partial y}
=\frac{GM_1y}{\rho_1^3}
+\frac{GM_2y}{\rho_2^3}
-\omega^2y.
\]
Thus the equations can be written compactly in terms of \(U\).

So the final answer is \(\boxed{\displaystyle \frac{d^2x}{dt^2}=-\frac{\partial U}{\partial x}+2\omega\frac{dy}{dt},\qquad \frac{d^2y}{dt^2}=-\frac{\partial U}{\partial y}-2\omega\frac{dx}{dt}}\).

